WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.000
The major thrust here is that wood is permitted only for fuel. Things which are not fuel are

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:48.000
permitted. So it's first kullam, you take a piece of wood and you use it as a lamp. That's number one. Furthermore, the hasher, talking about the hasher, not like a practice today, but the hasher is made of

00:00:49.000 --> 00:01:11.000
there's no ceramics or anything like that. In other words, if there's branches, little branches of wood, you could be megabeb in a hasher and use it for fuel. So therefore there are two kullot. Number one, you could use for

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:18.000
use it as a toothpick. Second, you could be megabeb in a hasher and use it as a toothpick.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Talking about toothpicks, you could take wood from the floor?

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
No, you would take wood from the fire, or whatever it is.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.000
A pile of wood that was set on fire?

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Yes, for fire.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.000
That wood was muksat?

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:41.000
No, that is only for fire. That's the point. In other words, the wood is only for fire, for fuel.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.000
That's what they plan on using the wood for?

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:56.000
No, the hachamim, the wood as a non-muksat object, yes, for fuel consumption. If you want it as a toothpick, then you have to be a hasher.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:05.000
What about, let's say, as a common practice in your home, you would set up a long board between two chairs or something so you could have a bench?

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Yes.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.000
So the wood then has another purpose?

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Because you specify it by your actions.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.000
So this is an unspecified wood?

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:27.000
Yes, that's right. Some wood is for fuel. Now you want to take a piece, I don't know how you call it, a splinter, you want to take a splinter and use it as a toothpick.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.000
So, that's gula number one.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Gula number two, that you could be a mekaber min hachaser.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Hachaser is what is outside the house, outside the edifice, the bedroom.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:54.000
And, um, shekoma sheba hasher mukham, that's the theory.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Teko min mekaber min hachaser veol umadlik.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Hach min mekaber min nevarach umadlik.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:14.000
He could do it, yes, only from what he has in front of him.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:21.000
Only what he has inside the house, not what he has outside.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:26.000
The problem is in the rationality.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.000
First I'm going to tell you the law, and then it goes into rationality.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Lo yitiro hachamin lakachat gisam lenakot poshinav.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
And according to them, it's only me'ebus shel vehema velvat.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Why? Because you're permitted to give food to the vehema.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Whatever is eatable for the vehema is not mukse, you use it yourself.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
But from a splinter, from the wood, from the stum wood, which is for hasakam, this is going to die out.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.000
And you can't eat it yourself.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:09.000
But if there was food for the vehema, and there was no food for the vehema,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.000
it's permitted to eat it yourself.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.000
And I'm going to tell you a story.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.000
I'm going to tell you a story now.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.000
There was a man called Hachamin.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.000
He had no knowledge of the law.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.000
He said, look, what I'm going to tell you.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.000
The word anyan, in Arabic, maknam.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:38.000
It's always not the grammatical nashon, but the semantic.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Now here it says, here the mishrash says,

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.000
ahme gabehme lef arafu madhid.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.000
The rasol is not there.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.000
Arababu will not change the rasol.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Because that's the kabbalah, the words of it.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:56.000
But anyano,

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.000
you understand what it says?

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.000
It's meaning is, semantically,

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.000
im bisher besadeh,

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.000
it means like this.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:12.000
That if you cook in the sadeh, not in the house,

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.000
then, and then alone,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.000
me gabehme masher besevigo umadlik.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.000
And he's explaining now the opinion of Rabbi Lezzet?

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.000
No.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.000
No, I understand.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.000
But is he explaining us the opinion of Rabbi Lezzet regarding these things?

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:29.000
No.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:32.000
The af is the Hachamin, not Rabbi Lezzet.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.000
But even before?

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.000
Yes, before it was the opinion of Rabbi Lezzet,

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.000
which is not an af.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.000
The only thing that you could use for a topic

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:44.000
is from ma'akhal behema.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.000
But they're using the word af.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.000
Wouldn't this necessarily be inclusive of what was before?

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:54.000
No.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.000
But the halakha is not going to be included.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.000
Saying that they are excluding that section,

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.000
and only including this section?

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.000
Yes.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.000
And therefore, the halambam doesn't tell you that this,

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.000
he doesn't say this is anyano.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.000
You understand?

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Even that rasol was actually put aside?

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Put aside.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.000
The halakha was anyano.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:14.000
Anyano.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.000
Yes.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.000
So therefore, rather than,

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.000
you see, that's the point,

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.000
because other people changed the rasol of the mishnah.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.000
So that's not discovered, halambam.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.000
They tell you the halakha is not like this,

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.000
and according to the halakha,

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.000
which is the way the hachamin in the Talmud,

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.000
this is the anyano.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.000
Although, if you read it on the basis of grammar,

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.000
it's inclusive.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.000
Alright?

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:46.000
But, probably, I'm just guessing,

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.000
the hachamin decided,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.000
in ahak rabbi l'izan,

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.000
later on,

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.000
in rabbi nakadosh time,

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:57.000
and they wanted to exclude this,

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.000
to explain it,

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.000
about the sadeq.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.000
Very often, when we've gone ahead,

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.000
and we're talking about the second gear solot now,

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.000
we've always referred to it in the time of rabbi nakadosh.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.000
Yes.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.000
Is it possible that many of these halakha changed

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.000
during the time of rabbi nakadosh?

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:12.000
No.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.000
You cannot change it.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:14.000
The only one who could change it,

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.000
is rabbi nakadosh.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.000
Meaning, at the time,

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.000
at the time of the betim finished,

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:20.000
of rabbi nakadosh.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.000
That's it.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.000
That was it.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.000
There was no changes made.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.000
Whatever the final,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.000
whatever the final svarot were,

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:27.000
for this mishnayot,

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.000
and all this,

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.000
the midur abat,

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:30.000
that was it.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.000
After that, there was nothing.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.000
The rabbi nakadosh,

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.000
I'm not permitted to,

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.000
they say something,

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.000
it was a question,

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.000
I don't interpret it the way everybody expects.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.000
They say in a few places,

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.000
it says,

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.000
rab tanahu,

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.000
to value.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.000
Because rab,

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:48.000
was a member of the bedim of rabbi nakadosh.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.000
And people explain it,

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.000
that,

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:54.000
who is halak,

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.000
is a tanah halak.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.000
That's not the way,

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.000
that's not the way to explain it.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:00.000
Now,

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.000
according to these people,

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.000
rab could have made a change.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.000
I,

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:06.000
I reject this.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.000
So,

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:08.000
Abba always said,

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.000
rab ben nakadosh ku bedim no.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.000
And rab,

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.000
although he,

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.000
could consider that tanah,

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.000
but he said,

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.000
he couldn't be a halak al bedim.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.000
I'm giving up that.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.000
So he explained,

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:19.000
rab tanahu balim,

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:21.000
it's much simpler than all this.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.000
You will bring me,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.000
a baraitan,

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:28.000
and your baraitan reads,

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:29.000
ABCD,

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:30.000
whatever it is.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.000
Rab tanahu balim,

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.000
he has a different baraitan.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.000
He reads the baraitan differently.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.000
Because he was a tanah.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:39.000
In the sense that he has the authority to,

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.000
press on,

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.000
authoritative,

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.000
formula of transcribante.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:45.000
He could tell you,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:46.000
a tanah.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.000
You may be,

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.000
this tanu rabbanaan,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:49.000
tanah,

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:50.000
rab tanahu balim,

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.000
and bring you another baraitan.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.000
Meaning he brings a baraitan which is authoritative,

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:54.000
while you brought a tanu rabbanaan,

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:55.000
which is a baraitan,

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.000
which is not necessarily a baraitan.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.000
That's right.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.000
And,

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.000
and I'm not too proud to bring this,

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:00.000
this,

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.000
because he is a tanah also.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:03.000
Right,

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.000
that's how you know that he determines,

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:05.000
the authority of baraitan.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:06.000
That's right.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.000
So,

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:08.000
so now that,

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:09.000
that is,

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.000
yeah,

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.000
I don't believe that he's holy,

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.000
but in,

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.000
nobody,

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:14.000
certainly not of rabbinical laws,

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:15.000
he cannot.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.000
So if he brought a baraitan,

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.000
it wasn't his own opinion,

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:18.000
or his own will.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.000
That's right.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:20.000
Rab tanahu,

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:21.000
rab tanahu balim.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.000
Yes,

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.000
he's not a,

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:24.000
he is a tanah.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:25.000
In other words,

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:26.000
he has the,

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:27.000
the authority,

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.000
to press,

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.000
baraitan,

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:30.000
our authority to,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.000
how do you know?

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:32.000
Rab tanahu balim.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:33.000
That's,

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:34.000
you know,

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:35.000
it means,

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.000
it was taught by him.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:37.000
Yes,

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:38.000
that's right.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:39.000
You,

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.000
I mean,

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:41.000
we was discussing,

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:42.000
you bring a baraitan,

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.000
and comes,

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.000
and,

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:45.000
well,

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.000
disagrees.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.000
Why?

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:48.000
Because he has his own tanah,

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.000
his own baraitan.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:50.000
Yeah,

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:51.000
that's it.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.000
So,

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:53.000
therefore,

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.000
here,

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.000
probably,

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:56.000
the hachamim,

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.000
decided,

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.000
not to make a hacha,

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.000
and,

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:00.000
therefore,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.000
they explained this,

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.000
for,

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:03.000
only for the sadev,

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.000
when you're cooking outside,

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.000
in your house,

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:06.000
and that's what a rabban says,

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:07.000
and you know.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.000
Clear?

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.000
Hachamim.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:10.000
Everything,

